  "processObserver": {
    "method": "docker top every 100 ms. Each process is named by the executable its argv runs, not by comm: the Platform Package launches every tool through the bundled loader, so comm reports ld-linux-x86-64 for a packaged FFmpeg and ld-musl-x86_64. for a packaged yt-dlp.",
    "processesAreNotRequests": "One packaged yt-dlp invocation is two processes, the bundled-loader bootloader and the PyInstaller child it forks, both carrying the same argument vector. ytDlpProcessPeak 20 is ten concurrent requests.",
    "ffmpegThreadRestrictionDirectlyObserved": "This run proves the restriction from FFmpeg itself, not from the yt-dlp command lines. The lane sampled packaged FFmpeg processes working on media in both windows — ffmpegProcessPeak 3 under load and 1 in the probe, across 58 probe observations — every one of them carrying -threads 1, and ffmpegUnrestrictedCommandLines is empty.",
    "unwrittenArgvReadsAreNotPasses": "Unwritten-argv reads are reported separately and never folded into the restriction counts. This run read none: ytDlpArgvUnwrittenTotal 0, ffmpegArgvUnwrittenTotal 0, unattributedArgvUnwrittenTotal 0.",
    "invocationsWithoutMediaWork": "The Toolchain Attestation probes the packaged FFmpeg with -version on first use in each main and worker process. Those processes open no input and do no media work, so ADR 0019 does not reach them: ffmpegWithoutMediaInputTotal 2 are excluded from the restriction counts in both directions."
  }
